The bitfield clear mask must not depend on the width of the compiler that
built micro-c.

EXPERIMENT-zzq fixed this block once: `1 << unit_bits` is not all-ones when
unit_bits is 64, because the shift count is taken modulo the register width, so
the clear mask came out ZERO and writing one bitfield wiped every other field
sharing the unit. The guard it added was

    if(unit_bits >= register_size * 8)   -> no mask needed
    else                                 -> & ((1 << unit_bits) - 1)

THAT GUARD ASKS ABOUT THE WRONG WIDTH. `1 << unit_bits` overflows according to
the width of the int in THIS source -- in whichever compiler built micro-c --
not according to the target's register_size. gcc gives that int four bytes and
stage 2 gives it eight, so the same line produced different masks depending on
who compiled the compiler, and nothing anywhere said so.

It became visible the moment `int` in the LANGUAGE became four bytes (zzw): a
bitfield unit is then 32 bits, `32 >= 64` is false, the else branch runs,
`1 << 32` overflows a 32-bit int, and the mask is zero again. The identical
defect, one width down, inside the code that documents it.

The intermediate is now a long -- eight bytes under both compilers -- so the
shift can never reach its own width. unit_bits is at most 64, and 64 takes the
branch that does not shift at all.

--- a/cc_core.c
+++ b/cc_core.c
@@ -3918,14 +3918,32 @@ void expression(void)
 			 *
 			 * A full-width unit needs no masking at all: ~(mask << off) is
 			 * already exactly the bits to keep. */
-			int bf_clear;
-			if(unit_bits >= register_size * 8)
+			/* AND THE WIDTH THAT OVERFLOWS IS THE COMPILER'S, NOT THE
+			 * TARGET'S. The guard above compared unit_bits against the
+			 * TARGET's register width, but `1 << unit_bits` overflows
+			 * according to the width of the int in THIS source, in
+			 * whichever compiler built micro-c. gcc gives that int four
+			 * bytes and stage 2 gives it eight, so the same line produced
+			 * different masks depending on who compiled the compiler --
+			 * and once `int` became four bytes in the LANGUAGE, unit_bits
+			 * became 32, the else branch was taken, `1 << 32` overflowed a
+			 * 32-bit int, and the clear mask came out zero again. Exactly
+			 * the defect this block already documents, one width down.
+			 *
+			 * A long is eight bytes under both, so the shift can never
+			 * reach its own width: unit_bits is at most 64, and 64 takes
+			 * the branch that does not shift at all. */
+			long bf_all;
+			long bf_clear;
+			if(unit_bits >= 64)
 			{
 				bf_clear = ~(bf_mask << bf_off);
 			}
 			else
 			{
-				bf_clear = (~(bf_mask << bf_off)) & ((1 << unit_bits) - 1);
+				bf_all = 1;
+				bf_all = (bf_all << unit_bits) - 1;
+				bf_clear = (~(bf_mask << bf_off)) & bf_all;
 			}
 
 			common_recursion(expression);
